From 16e2472caf83083b9664c0cd90c2ddd1a2692f89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bmh129 <11478682+bmh129@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:24:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Linux: GNOME-style window buttons in the title bar on
 Wayland (#78)

GNOME's Wayland compositor draws no window frames (it offers no
zxdg_decoration_manager_v1), so with the native Wayland backend winit drew
its own fallback frame above WordCraft's title bar, ignoring GNOME's theme,
dark mode and button layout. WordCraft now asks the compositor at launch
(apps/wordcraft/src/decorations.rs) and, where it draws no frames, turns
winit's frame off and makes the title bar the header bar, as in GNOME's own
apps (crates/ui-egui/src/window_controls.rs), as GridCraft and PdfCraft do:

- Minimize, maximize/restore and close in Adwaita's style (round buttons,
  symbolic glyphs drawn in code, no new assets), in the order and on the
  side named by org.gnome.desktop.wm.preferences button-layout, read
  through the XDG Desktop Portal with a 2 s timeout. Hidden in full screen.
  File (the Backstage) covers the title bar, so it gets a bar of its own
  carrying the buttons and dragging the window.
- Resize zones along the window's edges and L-shaped corners, above the
  rest of the UI; a hairline outlines the window while it isn't maximized.
- File > Options > General > Use system title bar (shown only where the
  app draws the buttons) gives the window back winit's frame at once,
  without a restart; read before the window opens, so it opens with the
  right frame. `ui.systemTitleBar`; saved as `systemTitleBar`.
- WORDCRAFT_TITLEBAR=app|system overrides the detection.

KDE, Sway, Hyprland, COSMIC, X11 and WORDCRAFT_X11=1 keep the frame their
desktop draws; macOS and Windows are unchanged. The new labels are in all
22 catalogs.

Not done: right-clicking the title bar doesn't open GNOME's window menu,
the window has no shadow or rounded corners, and the resize zones lie just
inside the window rather than outside it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
